                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="962-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ></span>Kel Audio Song Sparrow</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Cardioid Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The Song Sparrow is a large-diaphragm condenser microphone with a fixed cardioid pickup pattern. It is intended as a vocal mic, but has applications for instrument miking too.</p><p>The mic employs an edge-terminated, 34mm (O.D.) capsule voiced to have an open top end. It has a 26.5mm diaphragm made of 3-micron Mylar.</p><p>The amplifier circuit is similar to that of the HM-7U, albeit without the filter or pad. Note that the HM-7U uses a K47-style capsule, whereas the Song Sparrow uses a CK12-style capsule.</p><p>Both mics use high-grade components, including Wima film capacitors. </p><p>As with all the Kel Audio mics, the Song Sparrow uses a transformerless, low-noise output circuit.</p><p>The Song Sparrow has a flat frequency response between 100Hz and 3kHz, and a smooth presence peak above 3kHz, peaking at +5dB at 9&ndash;10kHz. Similarly, there is a +5dB peak at 40Hz, due in part to proximity effect (measured at 0.5m).</p><p>The mic is scheduled to ship in April, 2010. It will include a shockmount and an aluminum flight case.<p>Permalink: <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Kel-Audio/Song-Sparrow">Kel Audio Song-Sparrow</a></p><p>The mic was released in 2010.</p>
